new Employee()
      │
      ▼
  TRANSIENT
      │
   persist()
      │
      ▼
   MANAGED
      │
      │ setName()
      ▼
 DIRTY CHECKING
      │
    flush()
      │
      ▼
   DATABASE
      │
   detach()
      │
      ▼
  DETACHED
      │
      │ setName()
      │
      │  ❌ Hibernate doesn't track
      │
    merge()
      │
      ▼
   MANAGED
      │
      │ setName()
      ▼
 DIRTY CHECKING
      │
   remove()
      │
      ▼
   REMOVED
      │
 flush + commit
      │
      ▼
   DELETE


One important correction to memorize

persist() and merge() are not the same:

entityManager.persist(employee);

means:

"Start managing this new entity."

Whereas:

Employee managed = entityManager.merge(employee);

means:

"Take the state of this detached entity and return a managed entity containing that state."

And:

entityManager.detach(employee);

means:

"Stop tracking this entity."

Finally:

entityManager.remove(employee);

means:

"Mark this managed entity for deletion from the database."

Extra interview notes:
- remove() only works on a MANAGED entity (detached → merge first, or find again).
- flush() ≠ commit(): flush writes SQL to DB inside the open transaction; commit ends the transaction.
- clear() detaches ALL managed entities in the persistence context (not shown above).
- After method ends successfully → Spring commits → final flush + COMMIT.